<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Wei</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Wei was a northern Chinese state that ruled from 535 to 557 CE, one of the successors to the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>. The Turkic leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bumin-qaghan">Bumin</a> allied with Western Wei and married a Wei princess. In 552 CE he destroyed the Rouran and declared himself Qaghan, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-wei.jpg" alt="Western Wei" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Wei_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bumin allied with <b>Western Wei</b> China, married a Wei princess, and in 552 CE destroyed the Rouran in a decisive battle, declaring himself Qaghan and establishing the first state to bear the name Türk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 534–535 the empire split into Eastern Wei and <b>Western Wei</b> under the same house, and in 550 and 557 the Northern Qi and Northern Zhou took their places, until Yang Jian founded the Sui in 581.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rouran also conducted wide diplomacy, exchanging embassies with the Northern Wei, the Liu Song, the Southern Qi and Liang, the Tuyuhun and western powers, and using marriage alliances with the Eastern and <b>Western Wei</b> to play the divided north Chinese states against each other.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 556 his nephew Yuwen Hu forced the last <b>Western Wei</b> emperor to abdicate in 557 in favour of Yuwen Tai's son Yuwen Jue, who took the archaic title Heavenly King of Zhou.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a></li></ul></section>
